== 3. The rule. If each model is right with chance 1-e independently, a disagreement moves the log odds by +L or -L, L = log2((1-e)/e), and nothing else. +1/-3 is symmetric at no e.
   It is the LLR of a different pair: under 'rival' it is the right one of a disagreement with chance a, under 'default' with chance b. Then up = log2(a/b), down = log2((1-a)/(1-b)). Solutions of down/up = -3, by the unit of a +1:
   unit 0.25 bits: a 0.8108, b 0.6818; so theta 2 is 0.5 bits, theta 3 0.8 bits, clip 10 2.5 bits
   unit 0.50 bits: a 0.8619, b 0.6095; so theta 2 is 1.0 bits, theta 3 1.5 bits, clip 10 5.0 bits
   unit 1.00 bits: a 0.9333, b 0.4667; so theta 2 is 2.0 bits, theta 3 3.0 bits, clip 10 10.0 bits
   unit 1.58 bits: a 0.9750, b 0.3250; so theta 2 is 3.2 bits, theta 3 4.8 bits, clip 10 15.8 bits
   unit 2.00 bits: a 0.9882, b 0.2471; so theta 2 is 4.0 bits, theta 3 6.0 bits, clip 10 20.0 bits
   unit 2.32 bits: a 0.9936, b 0.1990; so theta 2 is 4.6 bits, theta 3 7.0 bits, clip 10 23.2 bits
   unit 3.00 bits: a 0.9983, b 0.1248; so theta 2 is 6.0 bits, theta 3 9.0 bits, clip 10 30.0 bits
   unit 4.00 bits: a 0.9998, b 0.0625; so theta 2 is 8.0 bits, theta 3 12.0 bits, clip 10 40.0 bits
   As the unit shrinks a, b -> 3/4: the counter's drift a - 3(1-a) changes sign at a = 3/4, so what it tests is 'this rival is the right one in more than 3/4 of disagreements'.
